<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balash</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/484">484</a>–488</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Valakhsh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balash was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> from 484 to 488, succeeding his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i">Peroz I</a>, who died fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>. Facing an exhausted treasury and unrest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, he reached a settlement with the Armenian rebels that granted freedom of Christian worship and restored the Albanian monarchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vachagan-iii">Vachagan III</a>. He was deposed in 488 in favour of his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-i">Kavad I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balash.jpg" alt="Balash" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_the_Sasanian_king_Balash_from_Susa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Balash, in Middle Persian Wardakhsh or Walakhsh, was the brother of the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peroz-i">Peroz I</a>, who reigned from 459 to 484. In 484 Peroz was defeated and killed by a Hephthalite army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>. His army was completely destroyed, his body was never found, and four of his sons and brothers also died. The eastern cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and Marw, now came under Hephthalite rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukhra">Sukhra</a>, a member of the Parthian House of Karen, one of the Seven Great Houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, quickly raised a new force and stopped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a> from gaining more ground. The Iranian magnates, most notably Sukhra and the Mihranid general Shapur Mihran, elected Peroz's brother Balash as shah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Immediately after taking the throne Balash sought peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>, which cost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> a heavy tribute. Little is known about him. Eastern sources see him as a mild and tolerant ruler, and his tolerance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> gave him a good reputation among Christian authors, who describe him as mild and generous. He appears, however, to have been only a nominee of the powerful nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukhra">Sukhra</a>, the de facto ruler.</p><p>When news of Peroz's death arrived, the Iranian nobles of Sasanian Armenia, including Shapur Mihran, wished to go to the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> to elect a new sovereign, and this let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> under Vahan Mamikonian proclaim independence. Given the weakness in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, Balash did not send an army and made peace on terms that provided: existing fire-altars in Armenia were to be destroyed and no new ones built, Christians were to have freedom of worship, conversions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> were to stop and no land was to be given to converts. In 485 he appointed Vahan Mamikonian marzban of Armenia.</p><p>A few months later a son of Peroz named Zarer rebelled, and Balash, with Armenian help, put the rising down and had him killed. In 488 Balash, an unpopular figure among the nobility and clergy, was deposed after a reign of four years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukhra">Sukhra</a> played the main part in this and made Peroz's son Kavad the new shah.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Balash” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sasanian king <b>Balash</b> restored it around 485 in the person of Vachagan III, a member of the old royal family, as part of a wider settlement with the Christian Caucasus after renewed Armenian resistance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 488 a Hephthalite army beat the Sasanian army of <b>Balash</b> and enthroned Kavad I, and in 498 the king gave Kavad 30,000 troops to regain his throne in return for Chaganiyan; Joshua the Stylite records Kavad leading Hephthalite troops against the Romans at Theodosiupolis in 501–502 and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
